= Methodology

The research aims to meet the Genealogical Proof Standard of the Board for Certification of Genealogists. That means searching widely, citing every source, comparing the records with each other, resolving disagreements between them, and writing down the reasoning. A name in an online tree is a lead to records, never a record itself.

The source notes and bibliography sort sources into these groups:

+ *Primary records* — registers of baptisms, marriages and deaths, censuses, passenger lists, military records and contemporary newspaper notices. One record can hold both strong and weak information: an obituary is good evidence of a recent death, but its account of a birth sixty years earlier is a memory.
+ *Authoritative secondary works* — careful published research that cites its records, such as articles in peer-reviewed genealogical journals.
+ *Compiled secondary works* — county histories and published family histories. Where records from the time survive, these works are checked against them.
+ *Collaborative databases and leads* — online family trees and similar pages, used to find records.
+ *Family testimony* — family Bibles, letters, interviews and memories, each with its source named.

A source marked _Unclassified_ has not yet been sorted into one of these groups. The groups are only a rough guide. How far a detail can be trusted depends on who gave the information, how close they were to the event, and whether other records agree.

Each person in the register also has an overall rating. _Primary_ means the person's identity rests on at least one original record or careful published study. _Tree-link_ means sources of some kind are attached. _Memoir_ means the person is known so far only from family testimony. _Unknown_ marks someone not yet researched. A rating is a summary; disputed facts still have to be judged on their own evidence.

== How this book was made

Drafts of the stories were prepared with an AI assistant, working only from the records cited. Tamsin Penhallow read every statement against those records, and is responsible for the conclusions. Where an AI tool helped to transcribe a record, the source note says so.

== Reading the ancestor table

The ancestor table numbers everyone from Tamsin, who is no. 1. Jory has the same parents, so every number from no. 2 onward applies to him too. Nora joined the family by adoption, and for her the table shows her adoptive family. David is no. 2 and Karin is no. 3. Each person's father has twice that person's number, and the mother has the father's number plus one. So David's parents are nos. 4 and 5.

== About this sample

// cite-lint: ok — disclosure about the sample itself, not a claim about the family
Every source in this book is titled "Sample record (fictional)" and is said to be held by the Flyleaf Sample Archive, which does not exist. The documents shown as figures are facsimiles made with Typst for the sample. The photographs are public-domain pictures of unidentified people and places from the Library of Congress. They stand in for family photographs and do not show anyone in this family.
